The Bayesian Cramer-Rao bound on the mean squared error matrix for an unknown random vector is given by an inequality involving an inverse matrix. The vector consists of a compressible signal and hyperparameters with specified prior distributions. The matrix is block diagonal with two distinct blocks. The first block depends on the measurement matrix and noise variance, while the second block depends on hyperprior parameters. For the signal model in \eqref{signal_model}, the BCRB on the MSE matrix $\mathbf{E}^{\bm {\theta }}$ of the unknown random vector $\bm {\theta }= [\mathbf{x}^T, \bm {\gamma }^T]^T$, where the conditional distribution of the compressible signal $\mathbf{x}|\bm {\gamma }$ is $\mathcal{N}(0,\bm {\Upsilon })$, and the hyperprior distribution on $\bm {\gamma }$ is $\prod _{i = 1}^{L}\mathcal{IG}\left(\frac{\nu }{2}, \frac{\nu }{2\lambda }\right)$, is given by $\mathbf{E}^{\bm {\theta }} \succeq (\mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }})^{-1}$, where\begin{eqnarray} & \mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }} \triangleq \begin{bmatrix}  \mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }}(\mathbf{x}) 

&  \mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }}(\mathbf{x},\bm {\gamma })

\\ (\mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }}(\mathbf{x},\bm {\gamma }))^T 

&  \mathbf{B}^{\bm {\theta }}(\bm {\gamma }) 

\end{bmatrix} = \nonumber \\ & \begin{bmatrix}  \left(\frac{\bm {\Phi }^T \bm {\Phi }}{\sigma ^2} + {\lambda }\mathbf{I}_{L \times L}\right) 

